            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Custom Home Additions</h2>
              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What exactly is a custom built addition?</h3>
                  <p>A <strong>custom built addition</strong> is a tailored expansion to your existing home — designed around your specific layout, aesthetic, and functional needs rather than a pre-fabricated or cookie-cutter solution. Unlike a stock extension, every dimension, material, and design detail is planned from scratch. Common types include <strong>second-story additions</strong>, sunrooms, home offices, guest suites, garage conversions, and expanded kitchens. ProBrothers, based in <strong>Media, PA</strong>, specializes in seamlessly integrating additions with your home's existing architecture.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Is building a home addition cheaper than buying a bigger house?</h3>
                  <p>In most cases, yes. In the <strong>Greater Philadelphia and Delaware County</strong> housing market, purchasing and moving to a larger home involves realtor commissions (5–6%), closing costs, moving expenses, and often a higher mortgage rate. A well-planned custom addition adds the space you need without the transaction cost, and you keep the neighborhood, school district, and community you already love.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How much does a second-story addition cost in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>A full <strong>second-story addition</strong> in Pennsylvania typically costs <strong>$150,000–$350,000+</strong>, depending on the existing roof structure, foundation capacity, square footage, number of rooms, and finish level. Partial second-story additions (adding over a garage or wing) can start around <strong>$80,000–$150,000</strong>. ProBrothers provides a detailed structural assessment and transparent quote before any work begins.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do I need permits for a home addition in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
                  <p>Yes — all structural additions in <strong>Delaware County and Chester County</strong> require building permits under Pennsylvania's <a href="https://www.dli.pa.gov/Individuals/Construction-Codes-Housing/UCC/Pages/default.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Uniform Construction Code (UCC)</a>, and most will trigger separate electrical, plumbing, and HVAC permits depending on scope. ProBrothers handles all permit applications as part of project management. Starting construction without the proper permits can result in stop-work orders, fines, and complications when you eventually sell the home.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How do I add a home office to my house without a major build?</h3>
                  <p>A <strong>home office addition</strong> doesn't always require a full structural expansion. Options include converting a garage, finishing an attic, or enclosing a screened porch. ProBrothers can assess your home's existing footprint to identify the most cost-effective route to a dedicated, productive workspace. Custom built-in office storage, proper insulation, and separate electrical circuits are key elements for a functional home office.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What is an in-law suite and what does it typically include?</h3>
                  <p>An <strong>in-law suite</strong> (also called a guest suite or accessory dwelling unit) is a self-contained living space within or attached to the main home. A typical in-law suite includes a private bedroom, a full or half bathroom, and often a small kitchenette or living area. They're popular in <strong>Delaware County</strong> as a cost-effective alternative to assisted living, and can also generate rental income. ProBrothers builds both attached and detached in-law suite additions.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How long does a custom home addition take from start to finish?</h3>
                  <p>Timeline varies significantly by scope. A <strong>mudroom addition or bathroom add-on</strong> might take 6–10 weeks. A <strong>guest suite</strong> runs 10–18 weeks. A <strong>second-story addition</strong> can take 20–35 weeks from permit approval to completion. Lead time for permits in Delaware County municipalities typically adds 2–6 weeks before construction can begin. ProBrothers provides a full project schedule upfront and updates you at every stage.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What materials should I choose for energy efficiency in a new addition?</h3>
                  <p>For a new addition in the <strong>Mid-Atlantic climate</strong>, ProBrothers recommends: <strong>triple-pane or high-efficiency double-pane windows</strong> to handle Pennsylvania winters; spray foam or dense-pack insulation in walls and ceilings; an <strong>energy recovery ventilator (ERV)</strong> to maintain air quality in tightly sealed spaces; and LED lighting throughout. These choices reduce utility costs long-term and improve the addition's comfort across all four seasons.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Will a home addition increase my property taxes in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>Yes, typically. In <strong>Pennsylvania</strong>, adding square footage to your home will trigger a reassessment by your county's tax assessor. The increase depends on the size and nature of the addition. However, the value added to the home — both for resale and personal use — typically outweighs the tax increase significantly, especially in high-demand markets like <strong>Delaware County and the Main Line</strong>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Can a home addition be designed to blend with the existing architecture?</h3>
                  <p>Absolutely — this is one of ProBrothers' core strengths. Every addition is designed to <strong>match or complement the existing roofline, exterior materials, window proportions, and interior finish style</strong> of the home. A poorly integrated addition is visible and devalues the property. ProBrothers uses the same exterior trim profiles, siding materials, and interior millwork details so the addition looks like it was always part of the home.</p>
                </div>
